Async colouring reaches a timer's tick, wherever inlining put it.

html implements `timer` as an `effect` over setInterval, so the `@tick` body
ends up inside the callback the override hands to it -- a lambda, not a named
function. The body awaits an async native, so that arrow has to carry `async`.

The pass that recolours what it built collected the owner's named functions and
none of the lambdas they held, which emitted `setInterval(() => { ... await ...
})` -- text no bundler accepts, and the failure a golden shows and a grep for
the `async` keyword would not.

The colour stops at the arrow, and that is the second half of the same claim:
`__effect0_mount` *constructs* the callback and hands it to setInterval, which
is not awaiting it, so nothing above the arrow is async either -- not the
mount, not `__effect0_up`, not the settle. Colouring the chain was what the
Go targets could not survive: `handle = every(d, func(){ ...blocking... })`
became an async statement, and passAsyncOffload refuses one inside the `if`
its caller wraps it in.

`__effect0_unmount` stays sync: it clears the handle and never runs the tick.

deny out/none/html/index.html `(?m)^  async function __effect0_mount` -- handing a closure over is not awaiting it

deny out/none/html/index.html `setInterval\(\(\) =>` -- the callback awaits, so it is an async arrow

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"
import . "sngl:time"
import api "js:./api"

output {
    none {
        html
    }
}

component main node {
    var running = true
    var last = ""
    timer(interval=500ms, enabled=running, @tick {
        last = api.fetchHello("poll")
    })
    text(value=last)
}

window {
    main
}
-- api/index.ts --
export async function fetchHello(url: string): Promise<string> { return "data"; }
